Once Upon a Time in ASTREA
Translated by: Archeggshell of Transpoultryation
1
???: [――Pant wetter.]
With that, feeling as if he had heard a voice disparaging him, a tremor ran through the shoulders
of Heinkel Astrea.
His eyes downcast, he looked over the state of his surroundings in the dressing room. Those
perceiving him, and secretly gossiping about him―― were none. Nobody was doing anything
like talking behind his back.
That was only natural. The only ones present here were those whose qualification to enroll in the
Kingdom of Lugunica’s Guard had been recognized; true Knights who had resolved themselves
to not tarnish that honor.
In that case, whose disparaging voice had that been? ――Nobody met those conditions, save for
himself.
???: [Heinkel, we’re all headed to the Silver Shield after this, got any plans?]
Heinkel: [Huh? Ah, ahh, sorry. Tonight doesn’t work for me. The shopkeep… nevermind, it’s
nothing.]
Invited by a colleague after having finished training, Heinkel forced a smile and vaguely ended
the conversation. The colleague gave a brief response of “I see” before leaving the dressing room
with his comrades.
Watching those backs depart, Heinkel let out a deep sigh.
Heinkel: [The Silver Shield… how could I possibly show my face before Conwood-san?]
That which appeared in his mind was the face of the shopkeeper of the Silver Shield, a bar of
preference for those in the Guard―― he was a person with whom the Astrea Family was long
acquainted, and thus someone Heinkel had known since the days of his childhood.
With his current frame of mind, he did not want to encounter such a person, and he would not
know what to say. Hence, despite not having anything to do, Heinkel declined his colleague’s
invitation, and began to head home by his lonesome.
As he avoided the gazes of others and left the training ground, the neighborhood had already
grown quite dark.
The training ground of the Guard, adjacent to the Royal Castle Lugunica, was a place where
Knights without any other duties which needed attending would gather every day to strengthen
their bodies and mind by swinging their swords. Heinkel was no exception. Today, he had swung
his sword to the point that the blisters on his hands had burst, and had lost so much sweat that
his body felt light.
Through all of that accumulated effort, all of that sweat, he would mold his mind and body into
those of a Knight. And once his body and mind had reached a sufficient state, the long-awaited
moment would arrive―― or it should have.
Heinkel: […That would only be if I had the right.]
Looking down at the blood-stained bandage wrapped around his palm, Heinkel muttered in a
hoarse voice.
Feeling the sword inscribed “Astrea” weighing heavily on his waist, Heinkel’s steps proceeded
terribly slowly on his way back home. There was no mistaking that the white moon aloft in the
night sky also found Heinkel’s pace to be irritating.
Those steps of his, as if a snail’s crawl, suddenly came to a screeching halt. ――It was not that his
weak-minded nature had reached its pinnacle. The cause was a peculiar voice that struck upon
Heinkel’s eardrums.
???: [At~, last~]
With a faint liveliness to it, it sounded like a woman’s breathing.
On a street quite deserted in the night, Heinkel turned his head at the strangely enthusiastic
voice of a woman, widening his blue eyes in surprise. ――After all, the woman was on top of a
wall.
On the same street as Heinkel, she was taking dangerous steps atop a wall that was not very
wide.
Heinkel: [Be caref-!]
Woman: [Eh? Au-!]
The moment he raised his voice, Heinkel flustered to shut his own mouth. But, it was too late.
Her attention captured by Heinkel’s voice, the woman missed her next step in a grand spectacle.
Seeing the woman’s body tilting towards the other side of the wall, Heinkel momentarily forgot
about his responsibility and regret, and immediately placed his foot on the wall, forcibly pulling
the woman into an embrace as she was about to fall.
And then――,
Heinkel: [――――]
Catching a glimpse of golden hair gleaming in the moonlight, and wide, circular blue eyes up
close, he was captivated by their beauty, and his breath involuntarily caught.
As a result――,
Heinkel: [Shi――!]
He ought to have held his ground atop the wall, but the excess momentum from the woman
carried over as he held onto her, and they both fell down the other side. ――The other side of the
wall, into the stream running through the Noble District, an inappropriate splash resounding
through the silence of night.
――That was the first meeting between Heinkel Astrea, who had just formally become a Knight,
and Louanna Hartley, his future wife.
2
Woman: [I’m truly sorry about all this~.]
Heinkel: [No, it was because of my inadequacy. To fall down from such a height, it’s shameful.]
Woman: [You aren’t shameful or anything of the sort~. You’re spoolio~!]
Heinkel: [Sp-spoolio?]
Woman: [It means "super duper coolio"~!]
At the vigor of the woman, who clenched her fists as she emphasised that, Heinkel's eyes
widened, his appearance resembling a drenched rat.
The time was right after he had jumped out to save the woman as she was about to fall from the
wall, and had messed it up. He had just barely managed to fall into the stream first, and thus
had successfully lifted the woman to prevent her from breaching the water’s surface, but that
conduct was far too shameful to be appraised as “spoolio” or anything of the sort.
Sitting on a stone curb by the side of the stream, Heinkel wrung out his tunic whilst thinking
such things. And as Heinkel reflected on himself in such a manner, the woman dried his head
with her own hand towel,
Woman: [Even though it was all because of my carelessness, you went ahead and got so soaked
in my place…]
Heinkel: [Don’t worry about that. To be honest, it could've ended with neither of us getting
soaked. It was me who messed that up, so me getting soaked was just my punishment for that.]
Woman: [――? Punishment is meant to be retribution for sins, y’know? You helped me out just
now, so what could be the sin in that? This sort of thing is a misunderstanding on the part of the
one delivering the punishment. A mistake of someone in the heavens… for example, the Divine
Dragon-sama or something like that~.]
Heinkel: [That’s way too presumptuous, so I’d rather conclude that it’s my mistake…]
The hastiness of her words seemed as if it could lead to a direct confrontation with the Divine
Dragon if left unchecked, prompting Heinkel to faintly strain a smile. Overwhelmed by the novel
exchange with the woman, he realized he had forgotten to ask the things he ought to have asked.
Heinkel: [Let’s put off the matter of me being drenched for now. More importantly, there’s
something I want to ask you.]
Woman: [Yes? Yes. ――Ah! Nevermind~, I understand even if you don’t say it~.]
Heinkel: [Huh?]
Woman: [――I’m Louanna Hartley~.]
Placing a hand over her chest, the woman declared so with a somewhat confident expression.
Understanding that to be the woman’s―― Louanna’s name, Heinkel scratched his red hair.
Likely, she had intended to predict Heinkel’s question in her own way.
Heinkel: [Although, the thing I wanted to ask was the reason why you were up on that wall.]
Louanna: […Ah~! I-is that so? Of course. That makes sense. Actually that might've just been the
most natural thing to ask, um, y'know. This is quite embarrassing.]
Heinkel: [No! I was also thinking of asking you for your name! After all, it would be
inconvenient to talk to you without knowing your name!]
As he spoke, he felt pathetic, wondering if he could not have perhaps come up with a better
cover, but at Heinkel’s words, Louanna blinked her wide eyes, and then smiled.
Heinkel thought that smile would have surely perceived him as looking ridiculous, but.
Louanna: [You’re quite the clumsy person. It’s spoolio.]
Heinkel: […Those two things don’t exactly go together, right?]
Louanna: [No, they do go together~. In society, there's also weirdos who like spicy food, isn't
that so? In truth, it’s sweet things that go together with yummy, right? But, for those people,
spicy things are what go together with yummy. It’s the same thing as that~.]
Heinkel: […I don’t think it’s that rare for someone to like spicy food.]
Louanna: [Don’t tell me, are you also someone who prefers spicy food~?.]
Heinkel: [Nah, I wouldn’t go that far. I prefer sweet things.]
The exchange and dialogue being far too childish, even he himself was astounded. Or rather, he
had allowed Louanna to seize far too much initiative. Perhaps it was so that Louanna did not
wish to disclose her reason for being atop the wall to Heinkel. Normally, people did not walk on
top of walls. That was, so long as this seemingly harmless Louanna was not involved with some
sort of crime.
In that case, there was no way that Heinkel, being a Knight, could shut his eyes to it for the time
being――,
Louanna: [Ah, this won’t do~. The reason why I was on the wall, right? I haven’t answered that
yet.]
Heinkel: [Will you tell me!? That’s… a relief.]
Louanna: [Yes, I’ll help out~… To tell you the truth, I’m being chased by someone.]
He had thought it might be something she wished to hide, but Louanna immediately spoke out.
But, at her soft spoken explanation, Heinkel spoke “What?” as his cheeks tensed.
So it was a matter of being pursued by someone, and climbing on top of the wall in order to get
away? In that case, it was a rather reckless action, but since there was context behind that
recklessness, he could understand it.
As he thought so, Heinkel looked over Louanna before him.
Likely, she was either the same age as Heinkel, or one to two years older. Her beautiful hair, akin
to golden threads of the finest craftsmanship, stretched down to her waist, and her blue eyes,
shimmering as if inlaid with jewels, left quite the impression. Her facial features were soft and
lovely, and there was a certain ephemeral nature to her such that it seemed like her white skin
would melt into the night.
Only, contrary to her shapely appearance, the clothes she wore was a common civilian garb.
Considering that they were in the Noble District, there was a slight yet undeniable feeling of
out-of-placeness.
Perhaps he could presume her to be the mistress of some noble, but――,
Louanna: [I probably saw something that I wasn’t supposed to see. On the street, I saw a person
being attacked~.]
Heinkel: [Wait, isn’t that an extremely important detail!]
Louanna: [Indeed~. A gentleman had collapsed, and the person who caused it looked in my
direction, then, I ran as fast as I could to get away… I’ll reenact it now~.]
Heinkel: [You don’t need to do that. Please don’t lay on the ground. I already did my best to
ensure you didn’t fall in the water.]
His unfounded suspicion completely demolished, Heinkel pulled Louanna upright as she
attempted to lie down on the ground.
The matter was far removed from the likes of a quarrel between man and woman, and rapidly
became tinged with crime. While that began to capture his attention, on the other hand,
considering Louanna’s conduct until this point, he also thought this.
Heinkel: […By the way, how much of that story are you certain about?]
Louanna: [Of course, everything from start to finish~. Eh~, are you perhaps doubting me?]
Heinkel: [I don’t think you have any ill intentions, but even with the best of intentions, it's
possible to mistake things…]
Louanna: [Mm, I suppose so~. If you want to be that thorough, I can bring you directly to the…
I’m sorry. I’ve realized that doing so would be dangerous so it’s best if we don’t.]
Heinkel: [It’s remarkable that you realized that on your own, I think. Yeah, remarkable.]
He gave earnest praise to her self-restraint as she desisted right before getting too impulsive.
That being said, he could not just blindly accept Louanna’s comments without any evidence.
At present, the only thing that could be said for certain was that Louanna was acting
suspiciously on top of a wall in the Noble District in the middle of the night.
Hence, Heinkel was greatly troubled, and once he drained his woes, he said “Understood”,
Heinkel: [We’re in the same dragon carriage here. Let's go check to see if your story’s true.]
Louanna: [Eh~. But, you just said that it was remarkable that I realized it was dangerous by
myself…]
Heinkel: [I wouldn’t let you go by yourself, but I’m, y’know, see?]
As he spoke, Heinkel tapped the Knight’s sword on his waist.
As for how persuasive that was, he had more doubts than anybody else, but it was far too
deceitful.
However, upon seeing that, Louanna hummed “Mm-hmm”, and after humming a few more
“Mm-mm”’s,
Louanna: [Understood~, let’s do that. I was also worried about the person who got attacked.]
Heinkel: [Yeah, we’ve gotta check on him. Could you lead the way?]
Louanna: [Of course~. Let’s get going, Spoolio-san~!]
Heinkel: […Spoolio’s something that you just said on your own, it’s not my name.]
Louanna: [Huh~, was that so? In that case, umm, I’m sorry. Would it be alright if I asked your
name one more time?]
Heinkel: [Rather than one more time, I haven’t even given it to begin with. I’m… Heinkel.]
To the hurried Louanna, Heinkel hesitated to give his family name, and ended up not saying it.
It was not because the other person was Louanna. No matter who the other person was, he
simply did not want to say it.
Unaware as to his inner feelings, Louanna toyed with the name “Heinkel, Heinkel…” on her
tongue over and over,
Louanna: [It’s a spoolio name~, Heinkel-san~.]
Faced with her smile, Heinkel could not bring himself to tell her that the name was not one that
overlapped with the nuances of “super duper coolio”.
3
The place Louanna guided him to was a street that could be reached even without climbing over
the wall.
It was part of the Noble District, but a block that did not get much pedestrian traffic. The row of
houses was mostly composed of villas belonging to individuals with their own domains, and so
when the owners were not staying only their custodians would be living on the premises. Hence,
it was often used by women in positions or vocations that did not wish to draw attention, and
was often referred to as the "Rendezvous Avenue”.
When it came to walking on that road in the middle of the night, Louanna’s objective was
also――,
Louanna: [So, I work at a flower shop in the Commercial District, but on my way back after
delivering a flower order, I completely lost my bearings and ended up in this neighborhood.]
Heinkel: [Flower girl…]
Louanna: [Yes~, I sell flowers. Do you like flowers?]
Heinkel: [Eh, hmm. Relatively, I do like them, I guess?]
For a moment, Heinkel harbored a vulgar impression regarding the term “flower girl”, but the
pure gaze of Louanna that followed pained his chest, and he responded.
In truth, he had never thought about whether he liked or disliked flowers. Only, Heinkel’s
mother had a hobby of growing flowers, so flowers had been familiar to him ever since he was
born. In that sense, it was natural to have flowers around as a decoration in daily life for
Heinkel.
Louanna: [How spunderful~.]
Heinkel: […Super duper wonderful?]
Louanna: [Yes~. As expected, you’re quick to catch on, Heinkel-san~.]
They did not know each other well enough for her to have such expectations of him, but Heinkel
had correctly guessed what spunderful meant, so anything he said from here would be a futile
resistance.
Regardless, it seemed like the had finally arrived at the street in question, but――,
Heinkel: [――There’s nothing.]
On the street where Louanna had testified to somebody having been attacked, there was
nothing.
Laying eyes upon that, Heinkel frowned, and beside him, Louanna panicked. She jogged up
towards the street, and as she waved her arms and legs around,
Louanna: [This is it~, this is where it happened~. There’s no doubt it was here, where a
gentleman whose name I don’t know, was attacked with a sharp blade by a gentleman or a lady
whose face I didn’t see.]
Heinkel: [The assaultee was a man, and you don’t know if the assailant was a man or a woman?]
Louanna: [Yes. The attack also happened in an instant, and there was no big scuffle, rather it
was a schwabahhhh and a zhabaawam like the wind~! ]
Louanna used large gestures to accompany her explanations, but for the time being it seemed
like there was no doubt it all happened in an instant. Only, based on her way of speaking and her
lifelike expressions, Heinkel did not think that she was speaking without giving it much thought.
That being the case, he contemplated the possibilities of her having mistaken what she had seen,
or having gotten the street in question mixed up. Thinking that there might have also been some
other possibilities, Heinkel surveyed the surroundings, and realized something.
Heinkel: […No matter how you look at this, isn’t this street just way too clean?]
There was routine cleaning to maintain the scenery of the Noble District, but on a street like this
that saw little pedestrian traffic, it was difficult to imagine that so much effort had but put into
the cleaning such that not even a single pebble was lying anywhere around.
That was to say, there was a certain credibility to Louanna’s testimony.
Heinkel: [――Louanna-san.]
Louanna: [Please wait, Heinkel-san~. At this rate, I think you’ll end up seeing me as a liar. But
you’d be wrong. The gentleman had collapsed over here… I’ll reenact it now~.]
Heinkel: [You don’t need to do that. Please don’t lay on the ground. I believe you.]
Louanna: [Really~!?]
Pulling Louanna upright as she was about to lay down on the ground, Heinkel gave her a nod.
Then, turning his back on the eerily tidy street, he tugged on her arm.
Heinkel: [I’ll escort you back to your home tonight. For further details, you can report them to
the Guard tomorrow.]
Louanna: [The Guard… but, would they really listen to me?]
Heinkel: [They shouldn’t flat out refuse you. Plus, I’ll put in a good word for you.]
Giving that response to the uneasy Louanna, in his mind Heinkel asked him “Really?”.
Could he really make it so the Guard would not disregard her? Was it not possible that Heinkel’s
good word would end up backfiring, causing the credibility of Louanna’s testimony to be
lowered?
How much faith could be placed in the words of Heinkel Astrea?
Louanna: [Thank goodness…]
Heinkel: [Eh?]
Louanna: [I can rest easy knowing that Heinkel-san will be accompanying me. What a relief~.]
Heinkel: [――. Let’s get going.]
As she placed her hand over her chest in an exaggerated gesture of relief, he could not bear to
look Louanna in the face as she said that.
She was unaware of Heinkel’s identity. Therefore, he knew that those words were not spoken out
of any overabundant consideration or flattery. And knowing that, he could not bear it in the
slightest.
If Louanna were to hear some of the rumors about Heinkel, there was no doubt that she would
immediately realize that the sense of security she felt was nothing more than a papier mâché.
――When they arrived at Louanna’s home, it was a regular housing complex located within the
Commoner District.
The ground floor was a kitchen for communal use, and the floors above were comprised of
rooms where the residents lived; it was not anything that particularly stood out for a dwelling in
the Royal Capital.
Only, Louanna’s room stood out so much that anybody outside would be able to guess her room
on their first try.
Heinkel: [Um, is your room the one with all those flowers decorating the windowsill, Louanna?]
Louanna: [Waah, amazing~. How did you know?]
Heinkel: […Just a hunch.]
Louanna: [A hunch~. Spamazing~.]
Among the row of rather plain stone-built residences, being able to guess the room covered with
flowers that asserted themselves vividly did not seem like something worthy of being treated as
“super duper amazing”.
Nevertheless, it was decorated with enough flowers to be sold, so it seemed like it was true that
she was an actual flower girl who sold real flowers. ――Feeling the slightest bit of relief, Heinkel
cleared the vulgar thought from his mind.
At any rate, he had safely escorted Louanna back to her home――,
Heinkel: [Well, this is it for me. I’ll come pick you up tomorrow.]
Louanna: [Eh~, aren’t you going to come in?]
Heinkel: […You ought to be a bit more cautious around people. I’m a man, and we just met.]
Louanna: [But, Heinkel-san, you’re dripping wet… besides, I have a good eye for people.]
As Louanna firmly pounded her chest and declared so, Heinkel frowned.
On this instance, he wished to disregard the objective contradiction that such a thing depended
entirely on Heinkel, but even if he disregarded it, that was a statement that lacked any grounds
for believability. They had only known each other for a short time, but Louanna was exceedingly
defenseless. If she had somehow managed to avoid going through any bitter experiences with
her appearance and good nature, it was nothing short of a miracle.
Heinkel: [Firstly, it’d be rude to your lover, or your family.]
Louanna: [Anyone who would make an unpleasant face to you after you helped me out,
Heinkel-san, are not the kinds of people I would accept as a lover or as family~.]
Heinkel: [――. Setting lovers aside, family’s not something you get to choose.]
Louanna: [I guess that’s true~. But, I don’t have either of them, so c’mon, don’t be shy.]
Louanna vigorously tugged on his sleeve, and after hesitating for a moment, Heinkel shook off
her slender fingers. Shoving his palm towards the dissatisfied-looking Louanna as she let out an
“Ahh~”, he stopped her.
Heinkel: [It’s all the more if you don’t have either of them. I understand your philosophy, so
please also understand mine.]
Louanna: […Understood. It would’ve been rude to Heinkel-san’s lover and family~.]
Heinkel: [While I do have a family, I don’t have a lover. I’m sorry.]
Trying to soothe Louanna as she averted her gaze in a sulkish manner, Heinkel more or less
turned down her invitation.
Then, he more or less ushered the reluctant Louanna into the building, and ascertaining that she
had safely returned to her room by the light in the window―― Heinkel hid himself away in a
corner of the street.
Heinkel: [I don’t exactly know how useful my sense of unease might be, but…]
If his sense of unease regarding the unnaturally clean street where Louanna had witnessed an
attack was on the mark, there was more than enough possibility that Louanna was really being
pursued by someone.
He did not mention anything due to not wanting to frighten her, but thinking of the
hypothetical, Heinkel had decided to keep watch.
Though it was now after he had finished drilling at the training grounds, Heinkel was fortunately
confident in physical strength.
Were he to serve as the night watch for a night, there was no need to worry about his body’s
response.
Only, he thought he might have ended up worrying the members of his household by not
communicating and not returning home, but it was for the safety of the public. ――If he made
that excuse to himself, he could somewhat forget about his feelings of guilt for not wanting to
return home.
Heinkel: [There’s no place for me at home, nor anywhere outside. What the hell am I doing
here?]
As his damp body was chilled by the evening breeze, Heinkel slowly shook his head.
The discomfort of feeling like he had no place, was caused by a guilt that Heinkel was
remembering of his own volition. Neither his colleagues at the training ground, nor the
household members who had seen him off this morning, not a single person had done anything
like reproach, ridicule, or scorn Heinkel for his weakness and fragility.
The one who did those things more than anyone else was Heinkel himself. That was exactly why,
there was no escape for him.
No matter how desperately he fled, it would continue to follow him like his shadow.
In that sense, his meeting tonight had been rather convenient. If he could make an excuse by
doing something for someone like this, the self-harm enacted by Heinkel’s mind would subside a
bit.
The only problem was――,
Louanna: [――Um, will you come in after all? I can serve some warm beverages~.]
The woman known as Louanna Hartley would not so readily neglect the solitary Heinkel Astrea.
4
Louanna: [I was surprised. When I looked outside the window, I saw Heinkel-san hiding in a
corner of the street… I understood it right away. You were keeping a watch over me~.]
Heinkel: […It’s not really like I was keeping watch over you, I guess it would be more accurate to
say I was standing guard.]
Forcibly dragging Heinkel along to her room in spite of his resistance, he decided to nitpick one
the phrasing in Louanna’s language as she made tea. That said, given that he had been abruptly
discovered by her while he was keeping a stealthy lookout, how much persuasiveness did
Heinkel’s words really hold?
Heinkel: [I know it might not be my specialty, but to think I would be discovered so quickly…]
Louanna: [No, not at all, didn’t I tell you earlier? I have quite a good eye for people~.]
Heinkel: [That expression isn’t supposed to be for when you find someone who’s been hiding…]
Or perhaps she meant it as saying that she was skilled at discerning people who were unskilled
at hiding? Either way, Heinkel’s loss of confidence remained unchanged.
Ultimately, that also went for how easily he went along with being brought into Louanna’s room.
Louanna’s room was on the third floor of the housing complex, and arranged with modest
furniture and bedding, there was plenty of color from the flowers decorated all around. ――This
was Heinkel’s first time in the home of a woman who lived by herself, his first time in the home
of anyone living as a commoner for that matter, so he did not know if this sort of thing was
typical.
And not knowing that, Heinkel’s impression as he looked around the interior of the room
was――,
Heinkel: [It feels pretty cramped. Can you really live in a place like this?]
Louanna: [Ah, you might be asking that sort of thing, but this is actually on the tidier side… or
rather, I just don’t own a lot of things, but I pride myself on being good with space.]
Heinkel: […Being good with space, by that you mean…]
Louanna: [Like, people who make good use of all the space in their room. See, this area here or
that corner, don’t you think I’ve done it quite skillfully?]
Guiding his gaze towards the edge of the room where she had stored things away, Louanna
asserted her talents of space usage, but, Heinkel’s eyes were unable to evaluate the usage of
space or how skilled it was, so he could not meet her desires for praise.
At his reaction, Louanna smiled with a “Did I put you on the spot there?”, and placed a cup of
tea in front of Heinkel, who had quietly curled himself up like a cat in unfamiliar territory.
At the faintly warm fragrance, Heinkel remembered that his body was freezing cold, so he picked
up the cup.
Given the location, he did not have many expectations for the flavor of the tea, but――,
Heinkel: [――Are these… Valneau tea leaves?]
Louanna: [Amazing~, you can tell? How spoolio!]
Heinkel: [I don’t really think it’s particularly cool, no?]
Louanna: [Ah, the spoolio just now, was a spoolio with the meaning of “super duper smart and
not a foolio” .]1
The difference between the two spoolios was not something he could make out from Louanna’s
pronunciation.
Regardless, he was surprised that she had served tea brewed with Valneau leaves.
After all, Valneau tea leaves were quite the luxury item, and ought to have been somewhat out of
reach for the masses.
Louanna: [Those are some valuable tea leaves, so please be sure to savor them. I also only drink
it sparingly. Like when I have a guest over… so, this felt like the perfect occasion.]
Heinkel: […Whether you’re frugal or not, I can’t really tell at all, you know?]
Louanna: [A woman who makes no compromises when it comes to hospitali-tea, I’d be pleased
if you took it as that. Despite how things may appear, I was originally a child of nobility much
like Heinkel-san, after all~.]
Heinkel: [Coughk, gahgk!]
Louanna: [Wahh, H-handkerchief~! Did something startle you!? Was it me!? Was it the tea!?]
1 めっこい here being used as both めちゃめちゃカッコいい (super duper cool) and めちゃめちゃ賢い (super
duper smart). Obviously, the pun is hard to maintain in English, so the smart version I handled “smart and
not a foolio” to still work with “Spoolio”.
Heinkel: [Just you… coughk, how do you know I’m a noble?]
Taking the handkerchief presented to him, Heinkel inquired as he held down on his choking
throat. At his question, Louanna appeared to look at him in puzzlement, and,
Louanna: [I mean, the way you sit in that chair and the way you sip your tea, it was just way too
perfect. So I just thought that you were someone who went through a lot of rigorous etiquette
training.]
Being told so, Heinkel paid attention to how he was sitting, and how he was holding his cup.
Pinching the handle of the teacup, he had his other hand placed on the bottom to support the
